mptcp-bpf-weight-rr for Debian (server/VPS side)
=================================================

This is the server/VPS counterpart of the router's OpenWrt
mptcp-bpf-weight-rr package built by the same openmptcprouter-feeds
source tree (see that repo's ARCHITECTURE.md for the router-vs-VPS
split, and mptcp-bpf-dscp's identical README for the analogous
DSCP-scheduler split this one mirrors).

bpf_weight_rr distributes send bursts across ALL active subflows
proportionally to weight (smooth weighted round-robin) - every active
WAN still carries some traffic, just not equally. For the alternative
that always sends on the single highest-weight active subflow instead,
install mptcp-bpf-weight (a separate source/binary package) rather than
this one - installing both is harmless, but only enable (systemctl
enable) whichever one matches the router's
network.globals.mptcp_scheduler setting, since both ultimately write
the same net.mptcp.scheduler sysctl.

Configuration
-------------

This package only ships the scheduler itself. Install
mptcp-weight-manager (from the mptcp-bpf-weight source package - one
manager binary serves either scheduler, since both use the same
endpoint_weights/weight_remote_id BPF maps) to actually set weights:

    mptcp-scheduler-weight.sh set id <N> <weight>

<N> is the MPTCP endpoint id the router assigned to the WAN that should
carry that weight. On the router that id is network.<iface>.ip4table,
kept stable across reconnects (DHCP renewal, mobile reconnects) once the
router's mptcp/multipath script passes it explicitly to
'ip mptcp endpoint add ... id <N>' instead of letting the kernel
auto-assign one. Look it up on the router with:

    uci get network.<iface>.ip4table

The router's openmptcprouter-vps init script syncs this automatically:
whenever network.<iface>.multipath_weight changes, its
040-multipath-weight tracker hook calls set_mptcp_weight_vps, which
POSTs the current weights to this VPS's /mptcp_weight API route (see
omradmin.py) -- no manual copying needed, mirroring how DSCP's
dscp_remote_id sync works via /mptcp_dscp.

    mptcp-scheduler-weight.sh show

lists every currently configured weight, both by interface/IP and by
remote id.

Service
-------

mptcp-bpf-weight-rr.service loads
/usr/share/bpf/scheduler/mptcp_bpf_weight_rr.o, pins it (and its maps)
under /sys/fs/bpf/mptcp, and sets it as net.mptcp.scheduler at boot.
This changes the *system-wide* default MPTCP scheduler on this host -
only install/enable this on a VPS dedicated to terminating
OpenMPTCProuter multipath connections.

After building/installing a new .o (e.g. after editing the scheduler),
run:

    systemctl restart mptcp-bpf-weight-rr

Stopping the service switches net.mptcp.scheduler back to "default"
before unpinning, so it doesn't leave the sysctl pointed at a scheduler
name the kernel no longer has registered.

Requirements
------------

A kernel with MPTCP struct_ops scheduler support (BPF_STRUCT_OPS for
mptcp_sched_ops), CONFIG_BPF, CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF and CONFIG_MPTCP
enabled. This is a fairly recent kernel feature - check
'bpftool struct_ops register' actually succeeds if the service fails to
start (see 'systemctl status mptcp-bpf-weight-rr').
